@* Shared by the router's <NotFound> fragment (client-side navigation to an unknown route) and
   by NotFoundPage (a deep link the server could not route). Both paths show the same page.

   A dead end is the one page that has to offer a way out of itself: the three destinations below
   cover why someone is here at all - a mistyped slug, a link into the catalogue, or an API name
   they can search for. *@

<div class="status-page">
    <span class="status-code">404</span>

    <h1>Page not found</h1>

    <p class="page-lead">
        There is nothing at this address. It may have been a typo, or a link to a page that has
        since been renamed - the whole catalogue is one search away.
    </p>

    <div class="status-links">
        <a class="status-link" href="">
            <Icon Name="overview" Size="20" />
            <span class="status-link-text">
                <span class="status-link-title">Documentation home</span>
                <span class="status-link-desc">Every API, grouped by area</span>
            </span>
        </a>
        <a class="status-link" href="getting-started">
            <Icon Name="bolt" Size="20" />
            <span class="status-link-text">
                <span class="status-link-title">Getting started</span>
                <span class="status-link-desc">Install, register, call</span>
            </span>
        </a>
        <a class="status-link" href="browser-support">
            <Icon Name="shield" Size="20" />
            <span class="status-link-text">
                <span class="status-link-title">Browser support</span>
                <span class="status-link-desc">The whole catalogue in one table</span>
            </span>
        </a>
    </div>
</div>
